Here is a Rust port of your C header.

There are a few important differences and improvements to note in this port:

1.  **Reset Sequence:** Your C code (`printf("%s%s%s", fg_color, bg_color, text);`) **does not** actually reset the color at the end, despite your description saying it does. If you run that C code, the terminal will remain colored for all subsequent output. I have added the reset code (`\x1b[0m`) to the Rust version to fix this and match your description.
2.  **String Safety:** Instead of `char *` (C strings), I used `&str` (Rust string slices), which are safer and don't require null termination.
3.  **Escape Codes:** I used `\x1b` instead of `\033`. They are identical (both represent ASCII 27), but `\x1b` is the standard convention in Rust.
4.  **Newlines:** The C function loops `printf("\n")`. In Rust, we can do the same using `print!`.

### 1. Direct Port (Closest to your C code)

This implementation keeps the logic exactly as you described, just translated into Rust.

```rust
// color_print.rs

// Define the ANSI escape codes
const RESET: &str = "\x1b[0m";

// Foreground Colors
const BLACK_FG: &str = "\x1b[30m";
const RED_FG: &str = "\x1b[31m";
const GREEN_FG: &str = "\x1b[32m";
const YELLOW_FG: &str = "\x1b[33m";
const BLUE_FG: &str = "\x1b[34m";
const MAGENTA_FG: &str = "\x1b[35m";
const CYAN_FG: &str = "\x1b[36m";
const WHITE_FG: &str = "\x1b[37m";
const DEFAULT_FG: &str = "\x1b[39m";

// Background Colors
const BLACK_BG: &str = "\x1b[40m";
const RED_BG: &str = "\x1b[41m";
const GREEN_BG: &str = "\x1b[42m";
const YELLOW_BG: &str = "\x1b[43m";
const BLUE_BG: &str = "\x1b[44m";
const MAGENTA_BG: &str = "\x1b[45m";
const CYAN_BG: &str = "\x1b[46m";
const WHITE_BG: &str = "\x1b[47m";
const DEFAULT_BG: &str = "\x1b[49m";

/// Prints a single line with specific foreground and background colors.
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `text` - The text to print.
/// * `fg_color` - The foreground color code (e.g., RED_FG).
/// * `bg_color` - The background color code (e.g., BLACK_BG).
/// * `trailing_newlines` - Number of newlines to print after the text.
pub fn print_styled_line(text: &str, fg_color: &str, bg_color: &str, trailing_newlines: usize) {
    // Print FG, BG, Text, and RESET
    // Note: The original C code omitted the RESET, which is why I added it here.
    print!("{}{}{}{}", fg_color, bg_color, text, RESET);

    // Print the specified number of newlines
    for _ in 0..trailing_newlines {
        print!("\n");
    }

    // Flush stdout to ensure immediate output (optional but good for interactive tools)
    use std::io::Write;
    let _ = std::io::stdout().flush();
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_print_styled_line() {
        // This test just ensures the function compiles and runs without panicking.
        // It won't verify colors in a test environment usually.
        print_styled_line("Hello", RED_FG, BLACK_BG, 1);
    }
}
```

### 2. Idiomatic Rust Version (Recommended)

Passing raw strings (`&str`) for colors is error-prone. You might accidentally pass `RED_FG` as a background color. A more Rust-like approach uses an `enum` to ensure type safety.

```rust
// color_lib.rs

use std::io::{self, Write};

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub enum Color {
    Black,
    Red,
    Green,
    Yellow,
    Blue,
    Magenta,
    Cyan,
    White,
    Default,
}

impl Color {
    fn fg_code(&self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Color::Black => "\x1b[30m",
            Color::Red => "\x1b[31m",
            Color::Green => "\x1b[32m",
            Color::Yellow => "\x1b[33m",
            Color::Blue => "\x1b[34m",
            Color::Magenta => "\x1b[35m",
            Color::Cyan => "\x1b[36m",
            Color::White => "\x1b[37m",
            Color::Default => "\x1b[39m",
        }
    }

    fn bg_code(&self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Color::Black => "\x1b[40m",
            Color::Red => "\x1b[41m",
            Color::Green => "\x1b[42m",
            Color::Yellow => "\x1b[43m",
            Color::Blue => "\x1b[44m",
            Color::Magenta => "\x1b[45m",
            Color::Cyan => "\x1b[46m",
            Color::White => "\x1b[47m",
            Color::Default => "\x1b[49m",
        }
    }
}

const RESET: &str = "\x1b[0m";

pub fn print_styled_line<F, B>(
    text: &str,
    fg: F,
    bg: B,
    trailing_newlines: usize,
) where
    F: Fn() -> &'static str,
    B: Fn() -> &'static str,
{
    // You can pass closures or just use the enum methods directly
    print!("{}{}{}{}", fg(), bg(), text, RESET);
    for _ in 0..trailing_newlines {
        print!("\n");
    }
    let _ = io::stdout().flush();
}

// Helper to use the enum directly
pub fn print_colored_line<F, B>(text: &str, fg: F, bg: B, trailing_newlines: usize) 
where 
    F: Into<Color>, 
    B: Into<Color>
{
    let fg_code = fg.into().fg_code();
    let bg_code = bg.into().bg_code();
    print_styled_line(text, || fg_code, || bg_code, trailing_newlines);
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_enum_usage() {
        // Type-safe usage
        print_colored_line("Hello World", Color::Red, Color::Black, 1);
    }
}
```

### Usage Example (`main.rs`)

```rust
mod color_lib; // or mod color_print; depending on file name

use color_lib::{print_styled_line, RED_FG, BLACK_BG, RESET};

fn main() {
    // Using the Direct Port version
    print_styled_line("This is Red on Black", RED_FG, BLACK_BG, 1);

    // Using the Idiomatic version
    // print_colored_line("This is Blue on White", Color::Blue, Color::White, 1);
}
```

### A Note on Production Libraries

While implementing this manually is great for learning, in a production Rust project, you should consider using the **`colored`** crate. It handles terminal compatibility (e.g., Windows support via `windows-term` or `ansi_term` under the hood) and provides a cleaner API.

**Cargo.toml:**
```toml
[dependencies]
colored = "2.0"
```

**Usage:**
```rust
use colored::Colorize;

fn main() {
    println!("{}", "Hello".red().on_black());
}
```
